  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAs a large-scale trough axis develops across the West Coast over\nthe next 48hrs, ridging will strengthen across the Great Lakes\nand East Coast, ushering hot and humid conditions back into the\nregion. The probability of temperatures reaching the upper-80s\nand low-90s is elevated, exceeding 70% early this week, which\nwill contribute to an increased heat risk. Several climate sites\nmay tie, come within a few degrees of, or break record warm\nminimum temperatures Monday through Wednesday. \n\nLong-range models indicated that the ridge will weaken late \nweek and into the holiday weekend, reducing the probability of\nhot conditions.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAs the aforementioned ridge begins to break down (mentioned in \nKey Message 1), the potential for severe weather and heavy\nrainfall will increase across parts of the East Coast Wednesday\ninto Thursday. The area of greatest interest currently appears\nto be north of Pittsburgh, where the northern extent of the\nridge axis will interact with a passing trough over Canada. This\narea is expected to have the best potential for favorable strong\nvertical wind shear and instability, supported by the increasing\nwarm, moist southwest flow early in the week. \n\nAt this time, the Storm Prediction Center (SPC) has outlined a \n15% probability of severe storms Wednesday into Thursday for \nnorthern Pennsylvania. Should the ridge axis sag farther south \ntowards I-80, the risk area for severe storms could expand to \ninclude a larger portion of our region.",
